from review_schema import A

A('6:1.1,20.1','Buddhadeb — Calcutta Art Studio','R',{'Process':'Chromolithograph according to slide 20: colour lithographic printing, rather than a hand-painted Kalighat sheet. The slide does not supply the number of stones or impressions.','Composition & Motifs':'The frontal seated Buddha raises his right palm and rests the other hand at his lap. A lotus pedestal, decorated pillars and red curtain frame him. Small kneeling figures occupy lower architectural niches; distant spires appear at right.','Edition & Circulation':'A named indigenous studio places a Buddhist subject within the expanding popular print market. Edition size is not supplied.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'South Asian religious subject matter is produced with European-developed lithographic technology in a Calcutta studio.'},'chromolithography popular-print lotus',sig='religious/cosmological=The seated Buddha and devotional framing structure the subject.|social=The studio represents local control over image production.|visual/aesthetic=Modelled drapery, architectural depth and rich printed colour create a devotional presence.',dist='A European origin for the print technology does not make the religious subject European.|The opening and closing reproductions are the same print, not two works.')
A('6:2.1','Fort at Agra — Bacon, Stanfield and Kernot','R',{'Process':'Engraving, with fine linear shading across masonry, water and sky. The credits distinguish sketch, drawing and engraved realization.','Composition & Motifs':'The fort rises at left above a river; boats occupy the middle distance, and a small group of people sits or stands on rocks in the lower right. Wide sky and distant hills frame the building.','Edition & Circulation':'Multiple credited makers demonstrate the stages through which a view became a reproducible image.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'An Indian site is represented through a European reproductive print and picturesque landscape conventions.'},'engraving picturesque colonial-documentation',sig='social=The separate sketcher, draughtsman and engraver reveal collaborative production.|political=The print belongs to the module’s discussion of colonial documentation.|visual/aesthetic=Foreground figures and receding river organize the monument as a landscape spectacle.',dist='The title identifies the site; the print is not a measured architectural survey or a photograph.')
A('6:3.1','Unfinished textile woodblocks — Pedana','R',{'Process':'Printing matrices rather than paper impressions: carved wooden pieces retain raised outlines and partly drawn designs. The photograph shows several stages of preparation.','Composition & Motifs':'Separate floral scroll blocks surround a broader central piece with bird-like forms and an unfinished drawn pattern.','Edition & Circulation':'The named Pedana workshop supplies a living example of indigenous textile-printing skill; neither date nor production volume is given.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'The local textile-printing tradition must be distinguished from the later arrival of particular European paper-print technologies.'},'relief-print craft-agency kalamkari',sig='social=Naming Gangadhar and Narsaiah makes block preparation and workshop labour visible.|regional-identity=Pedana situates the technique in Andhra Pradesh.',dist='A woodblock is the printing matrix, not the finished cloth or a paper print.|The blocks are unfinished; not every drawn line has already been carved.')
A('6:3.2','Kalamkari printed cotton — Polavaram','T',{'Technique':'The caption specifies printed cotton. Repeated curling units support a block-print context; the image does not expose every mordanting or dyeing step.','Field & Border Organization':'A dense repeat of rounded and pointed floral scrolls covers a red field; the photograph crops out the complete textile edges.','Dyes & Materials':'Cotton is specified; cream, blue-dark outlines and red are visible. Exact dye chemistry is not identified.','Function & Circulation':'The cloth hangs for display in a workshop context at Polavaram, Andhra Pradesh; its intended finished use is not stated.'},'kalamkari relief-print floral-trade',sig='social=The workshop credit situates the patterned cloth in skilled local production.|visual/aesthetic=Repeated motifs create a continuous field rather than a central narrative panel.',dist='Kalamkari in this example is printed cotton; the term does not guarantee that every line was freely drawn with a pen.')
for refs,title,desc,other in [('6:4.1','Saint Sebastian woodblock matrix','The carved surface shows a central bound figure attacked by archers, with a dense text block below. The matrix has recessed areas around raised image and letter forms.','Saint Sebastian woodblock impression'),('6:4.2','Saint Sebastian woodblock impression','Black lines describe the central bound saint, archers and the lower text. The composition reverses the orientation of the neighbouring matrix.','Saint Sebastian woodblock matrix')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':'Relief woodcut. Raised areas carry ink, while cut-away areas remain unprinted; the paired matrix and impression make reversal visible.','Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'The lecture gives Europe but no specific location, maker or edition. Text and image share the printing surface.'},'relief-print print-transfer',sig='visual/aesthetic=The pair connects carved form to the resulting black-and-white image.|social=One matrix enables repeat impressions without redrawing the whole scene.',dist='Woodcut is relief printing, not intaglio engraving or lithographic planography.',compare=[{'target':other,'relationship':'complementary','reason':'Matrix and impression explain two stages of the same printing operation.'}],uncertain=['Exact object, maker and date are unspecified.'])
A('6:5.1','Lithographic stone — demonstration video still','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'A pale flat stone carries a faint dark image, with its thick rounded edge visible at lower right. The screenshot supplies evidence of the matrix surface, not a finished edition.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'Process-demonstration video still; the full motion sequence is not available in the PDF.','Composition':'The oblique close-up emphasizes flat surface and thickness, while the image on it remains too faint to identify securely.'},'lithography',sig='visual/aesthetic=Attention shifts from the printed picture to the surface that produces it.',dist='A stone with an image is not evidence of relief carving; lithography separates image and non-image areas chemically.',uncertain=['Image identity, maker and demonstration metadata are not provided.'])
A('6:6.1','Relief-printing demonstration','F',{'Subject & Evidence':'A hand peels paper from a small block with a bold black linear face. The lifted sheet exposes both the inked matrix and its impression beside a press roller.','Studio vs Documentary Context':'Contemporary process photograph. It illustrates a technique rather than identifying a historical artwork.','Composition':'The diagonal lifted paper makes transfer and reversal visible; the adjacent slide diagram concerns lithography and must be read separately.'},'relief-print lithography',sig='social=The hand and press show physical labour in image reproduction.|visual/aesthetic=The image clarifies the relationship between raised matrix and inked impression.',dist='The left photograph demonstrates relief printing; the right diagram on the same slide explains a different, planographic process.')
for refs,title,desc,proc in [('6:7.1','Sicri Gully — Hodges and Angus','Dense foreground trees and a winding path lead toward a pale distant hill. Tiny standing figures at lower left establish scale.','Metal engraving is captioned, translating Hodges’s painted view into fine tonal lines.'),('6:7.2','An Excavated Temple on the Island of Salsette','A dark rock-cut façade with rows of rectangular openings stands against a lighter rock face. Small figures and low structures occupy the foreground.','The embedded label calls it an engraving; a more specific process is not inferred solely from the coloured reproduction.'),('6:11.1','Part of the Kanaree Caves, Salsette','A low dark rock ceiling spans the view. Carved figures frame a central doorway; an arched opening appears at right. Two small visitors stand at the foot of foreground steps.','The slide calls it metal engraving; shaded tonal passages and colour are visible but do not alone resolve the exact combination of intaglio processes.')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':proc,'Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'These published views frame Indian places for distant viewers within the module’s discussion of colonial documentation.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Indian landscape or rock-cut architecture is the subject; European artists and reproductive print conventions mediate its representation.'},'picturesque colonial-documentation engraving',sig='political=The module situates the print within colonial ways of documenting places.|visual/aesthetic=Scale contrasts, framed viewpoints and tonal recession turn the site into a composed view.',dist='The print’s date is not the date of the landscape or ancient architecture it depicts.|A picturesque view selects and organizes evidence; it is not an unmediated record.')
A('6:8.1','Antiquities of India — title page','R',{'Process':'An engraved title image combining lettering, architectural fragments and vegetation.','Composition & Motifs':'A central stone-like tablet bears the title and dedication. Palm fronds rise behind it; carved fragments, a circular object and vegetation cluster at the base.','Edition & Circulation':'The page names Twelve Views and dedication to the Society of Antiquaries of London, presenting Indian sites within a British antiquarian publishing frame.','Text–Image Relationship':'The title is designed to resemble an inscription on an architectural object. The embedded imprint appears to read 1799 while the slide caption says 1808; these may refer to plate and issue dates and are retained separately.'},'colonial-documentation picturesque',sig='political=Institutional dedication locates the images within an antiquarian knowledge network.|visual/aesthetic=The title itself becomes an imagined archaeological assemblage.',dist='The surrounding fragments do not prove that all objects were excavated together at one site.|Do not silently collapse the image’s imprint and the lecture’s publication date.',uncertain=['1799-looking plate imprint versus 1808 caption requires edition-level verification.'])
A('6:9.1','Koummars — Solvyns','R',{'Process':'Coloured etching according to the embedded label; incised-line printing is distinct from a woodcut.','Composition & Motifs':'A standing man in pale wrapped cloth occupies the foreground. Behind him, a seated worker handles clay or a vessel beneath a thatched canopy; pots and upright poles locate a workshop.','Edition & Circulation':'The printed label identifies the subject categorically as Koummars; the lecture frames the series as colonial documentation of people.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Indian occupational life is depicted through a European-authored etching and classification system.'},'etching colonial-documentation craft-agency',sig='social=The image shows both an artisan figure and work setting.|political=The categorical title participates in colonial ordering of people and occupations.',dist='A labelled occupational type is not a statistically representative portrait of every person in that community.')
A('6:10.1','A Bazar — Solvyns','R',{'Process':'Coloured etching, with linear shading under restrained colour.','Composition & Motifs':'A street corridor narrows toward the centre. Seated sellers, standing buyers and large woven umbrellas fill both sides; a thatched stall dominates the right foreground and masonry houses rise behind.','Edition & Circulation':'The English label A Bazar and a secondary handwritten identification show how the scene was made legible for viewers outside the depicted market.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Calcutta market life is the subject; the representation uses a European print medium and viewpoint.'},'etching colonial-documentation',sig='economic/trade=Exchange is shown at the scale of everyday markets.|social=Different postures and activities populate the street.|visual/aesthetic=Perspective and repeated umbrellas organize a crowded scene.',dist='The print records an authored view of a market, not an exhaustive inventory of Calcutta commerce.')
A('6:12.1','Diospyros melanoxylon — Roxburgh botanical plate','R',{'Process':'Printed botanical plate in Plants of the Coast of Coromandel. The slide does not specify the exact printing or colouring process of this impression.','Composition & Motifs':'A branching stem with green leaves and a yellow-brown fruit fills the upper sheet. An isolated flowering stem appears at right; dissected floral, fruit and bark-like details form a separate lower row.','Edition & Circulation':'The Latin name and analytic arrangement belong to a published botanical knowledge project. Roxburgh is named; the slide does not separately identify the original botanical draughtsman or engraver.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A South Asian plant enters a European-format scientific publication; subject location and image-production labour should not be conflated.'},'botanical-illustration colonial-documentation',sig='political=The module includes plants alongside people and places in colonial documentation.|visual/aesthetic=Separate diagnostic details prioritize identification over landscape narrative.|social=The lack of a separate draughtsman’s credit is a metadata limit, not proof that Roxburgh drew every line.',dist='This is an analytical botanical plate, not a floral still life or a record of every plant part at natural scale.')
A('6:13.1','A woman strikes a man with a broom','P',{'Composition':'Two large figures occupy a nearly empty pale sheet. The standing woman bends over a kneeling man and raises a broom diagonally above her head.','Line & Hand':'Broad sweeping contours and economical shaded bands model bodies and sari folds without a detailed background.','Figural Style':'The woman holds the man near the head; his bent body and gathered hands contrast with her extended arm.','Palette & Pigment':'Black, white-grey and small red-yellow accents make the gesture legible.'},'kalighat popular-print social-satire',sig='social=The confrontational domestic scene opens a space for social commentary within popular painting.|visual/aesthetic=Economical outline and empty ground intensify action.',dist='This is a Kalighat painting, not a mechanically printed sheet merely because it appears in the printmaking module.')
A('6:13.2','A cat with a lobster — Kalighat painting','P',{'Composition':'An enlarged seated cat fills the sheet, holding a lobster across its mouth. Its tail curls along the bottom as a broad black mass.','Line & Hand':'Bold contours and graduated orange washes model the pale body; spots are large filled patches.','Motifs & Hidden Details':'A forehead mark and bead-like necklace give the cat human devotional attributes. Long lobster feelers and legs extend toward the left edge.','Palette & Pigment':'Warm yellow-orange fur contrasts with a blue-green crustacean and red accents.'},'kalighat social-satire animal-motifs',sig='social=The combination of devotional markers and predation supports a satirical reading, requiring contextual grounding.|visual/aesthetic=Large simplified forms remain instantly recognizable.',dist='The cat’s religious markers are part of a painted representation, not evidence of an actual animal’s religious identity.',compare=[{'target':'A cat with a lobster — Battala print','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Adjacent slides compare the same subject in painting and print, without proving one exact sheet copied the other.'}])
A('6:14.1','A cat with a lobster — Battala print','R',{'Process':'Battala woodcut context: relief printing replaces broad painted wash with patterned lines, stippled spots and repeated hatching. The slide shows a monochrome impression.','Composition & Motifs':'The seated cat carries a lobster horizontally in its mouth; its tail curls across the feet. The forehead mark and neck beads recur, while the body has many more small dark spots than the painted comparison.','Edition & Circulation':'The caption locates the image in nineteenth-century Calcutta popular print culture.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A locally circulating satirical subject crosses between indigenous painting and a reproducible print format.'},'relief-print popular-print social-satire',sig='visual/aesthetic=Linear pattern substitutes for the painting’s tonal wash.|social=Print reproduction offers another vehicle for popular imagery.',dist='The shared subject does not make the Kalighat painting and Battala woodcut the same object.',compare=[{'target':'A cat with a lobster — Kalighat painting','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Explicit sequential comparison of subject across media.'}])
A('6:15.1','Shiva playing a veena — Battala','R',{'Process':'Woodcut context, with dense black linework and red-green colour additions. The reproduction does not establish whether all colour is separately printed or applied by hand.','Composition & Motifs':'A seated multi-faced Shiva holds a tall veena at left. Serpent heads rise around the faces; a horn-like object is held at right. Draped curtains frame the top and a small reclining bull occupies the bottom compartment.','Edition & Circulation':'The caption identifies a nineteenth-century Battala print from Calcutta.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A regional devotional image uses the reproducibility of popular print; neither religious subject nor bright colour identifies the print process by itself.'},'relief-print popular-print shiva-iconography',sig='religious/cosmological=Multiple faces, serpents, instrument and bull differentiate the deity’s iconographic presentation.|visual/aesthetic=The frontal arrangement and border make the image resemble a portable shrine.',dist='Coloured patches extending beyond black outlines do not alone prove misregistration; hand-colouring is another possible explanation.')
for refs,title,desc,other in [('6:16.1','Mahant and Elokeshi — temple encounter','A seated bearded religious figure at left faces a woman and companions at right before a domed shrine. The central shrine contains a dark upright emblem. Curtains frame the upper edge.','Mahant and Elokeshi — raised blade scene'),('6:16.2','Mahant and Elokeshi — raised blade scene','A standing man raises a curved blade over a woman who crouches beside a low platform and lifts both hands. A patterned column and overhead curtain stage the confrontation.','Mahant and Elokeshi — temple encounter')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':'Monochrome Battala woodcut reproduced from a book; repeated small marks texture clothing and furnishings.','Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'The shared caption identifies the Mahant–Elokeshi episode, demonstrating popular print’s engagement with contemporary scandal as well as religion.'},'relief-print popular-print social-satire',sig='social=The narrative brings religious authority and domestic violence into public visual culture.|visual/aesthetic=Clear gestures and frontal staging support rapid narrative recognition.',dist='The two scenes are distinct images in a narrative sequence, not duplicate reproductions of one action.',compare=[{'target':other,'relationship':'complementary','reason':'Separate episodes grouped under one caption.'}])
A('6:16.3','Goddess Durga with her family — Krishna Chandra','R',{'Process':'Battala print with black linear pattern and restrained red-green colour. Exact colour application and block count are not established.','Composition & Motifs':'Multi-armed Durga stands centrally above a lion and a defeated figure. Other deities flank her in separate shallow niches, including elephant-headed Ganesha at far left. A broad semicircular ornamental arch encloses the group.','Edition & Circulation':'A named maker and Calcutta popular-print context connect devotional imagery with local production.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A Bengali devotional ensemble is distributed in printed form.'},'popular-print relief-print durga-iconography',sig='religious/cosmological=The goddess’s victorious central position and accompanying family define the devotional programme.|visual/aesthetic=The arched frame and pedestal organize multiple figures into a single shrine-like image.',dist='The side figures are part of an ordered family ensemble; this is not simply a solitary goddess portrait.')
A('6:17.1','Jazb al-qulub ila diyar al-mahbub — lithographed cover','R',{'Process':'Lithographed book cover. Dense curved calligraphy and ornamental borders exploit a process able to preserve handwritten forms.','Composition & Motifs':'A large central title field is framed by floral scrolls, with smaller horizontal text bands above and below. A modern Library of Congress label overlaps the lower printed area.','Edition & Circulation':'The slide gives Lucknow, 1916, and a printer name transcribed as Mushi aval Kishur; that spelling is retained as a source issue.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A text in an Islamicate calligraphic tradition is reproduced using lithographic technology.'},'lithography calligraphy',sig='social=Print can reproduce calligraphic conventions rather than replacing them with movable-type aesthetics.|visual/aesthetic=Writing and floral ornament structure the entire cover.',dist='The modern library barcode is collection metadata, not part of the 1916 printed design.',uncertain=['Printer transcription needs catalogue correction.'])
for refs,title,proc,desc in [('6:17.2','Tiger — Tugra lithograph','Lithograph, as captioned.','Calligraphic strokes form the tiger’s body, legs, face and high curling tail. Small plants fill the ground and a domed building appears at upper left.'),('6:17.3','Hawk — Tugra chromolithograph','Chromolithograph, as captioned; the number of colour matrices is not supplied.','Bold calligraphy forms a large hawk bending over a smaller bird. Pale blue sky and ochre ground surround the black graphic silhouette.')]:
 A(refs,title,'R',{'Process':proc,'Composition & Motifs':desc,'Edition & Circulation':'The captions locate these prints in Delhi/Lahore or Delhi and identify V&A examples.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Zoomorphic calligraphic imagery is reproduced with lithography; the depicted animal and written text are inseparable parts of the design.'},'zoomorphic-calligraphy lithography',sig='religious/cosmological=Calligraphic content may carry devotional meaning, but the inscriptions are not translated from the slide.|visual/aesthetic=Letters become animal anatomy while remaining visibly script.',dist='Tugra in this slide’s label refers to the calligraphic image; do not equate every such animal design with an Ottoman sultan’s official signature.')
A('6:18.1','Scene in Bombay — Grindlay, Reeve and Hogarth','R',{'Process':'The caption specifies etching, aquatint and hand-colouring, with separate drawing, engraving and colouring credits.','Composition & Motifs':'Palanquin bearers and groups of people populate a broad street in front of large masonry buildings. A resting figure and enclosed palanquin fill the lower right; a tall parasol marks the left group.','Edition & Circulation':'Multiple credited workers produce a reproducible urban view for circulation.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Indian street life is shown through European printmaking and a colonial urban viewpoint.'},'etching aquatint colonial-documentation',sig='social=Credit distinctions expose distributed production labour.|visual/aesthetic=Aquatint-like tonal fields and colour create depth and atmosphere.',dist='Hand-colouring is applied to an impression; it is not the same operation as printing each colour from a lithographic stone.',compare=[{'target':'Vijaya — Annoda Prosad Bagchi','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Slide 18 pairs distinct print technologies and makers.'}])
A('6:18.2','Vijaya — Annoda Prosad Bagchi','R',{'Process':'Hand-tinted chromolithograph, explicitly combining colour printing and additional manual tinting.','Composition & Motifs':'A bearded ascetic figure carrying an elephant-headed child stands at left. A crowned woman occupies the centre, surrounded by women; one touches her face. A small plant pot, tray and seated woman anchor the foreground.','Edition & Circulation':'Calcutta Art Studio and Annoda Prosad Bagchi are named, foregrounding local authorship and studio production.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'A South Asian religious family narrative is represented with lithographic technology and modelled, receding space.'},'chromolithography popular-print',sig='religious/cosmological=Divine figures are presented through an intimate group interaction.|social=The named artist and studio complicate a narrative of technology flowing only from foreign makers.|visual/aesthetic=Modelling and spatial depth differ from the flatter Battala examples.',dist='A chromolithograph can also receive hand-tinting; the two descriptions are compatible.',compare=[{'target':'Scene in Bombay — Grindlay, Reeve and Hogarth','relationship':'parallel','reason':'Explicit side-by-side comparison of authorship and print processes.'}])
A('6:19.1','Kali and Tara — Calcutta Art Studio','R',{'Process':'Colour print attributed to Calcutta Art Studio; the museum record is the check for exact lithographic process. The reproduction does not reveal a stone count.','Composition & Motifs':'The sheet is split vertically into two goddess images. Kali at left has four arms, loose dark hair, a garland of heads, a blade and severed head, with reclining bodies below. Tara at right carries a lotus, blade and bowl-like object; a skull garland, skull crown, tiger-skin garment and snakes distinguish her. Flames rise on both sides of Tara’s feet, with a pale reclining body beneath. A loose skull and bones appear along the lower left.','Edition & Circulation':'Names in multiple scripts and studio/copyright lines appear on the bottom margin, making the sheet a published devotional commodity.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'Bengali religious iconography is reproduced through a modern colour-print enterprise.'},'chromolithography popular-print kali-tara',sig='religious/cosmological=The paired arrangement invites comparison of related but distinct goddess iconographies.|economic/trade=Studio imprint and copyright text mark a commercial printed image.|visual/aesthetic=Shading and atmospheric backgrounds coexist with frontal iconographic clarity.',dist='Kali and Tara are not interchangeable labels for one repeated figure; compare attributes, garlands, ground and body treatment.|The two halves belong to a single sheet.')
A('6:21.1','Mahavir — Chitrasala Studio','R',{'Process':'A coloured popular print; the caption names Chitrasala Studio but does not explicitly state the process, so colour alone is not used to certify chromolithography.','Composition & Motifs':'A richly crowned, jewelled seated figure occupies a symmetrical throne. Standing attendants hold fly-whisks and flowers; two winged figures above pour streams from vessels. A sun-face and crescent-like emblem flank the central head; a small lion-like mark appears on the pedestal front.','Edition & Circulation':'The Pune studio location extends the module beyond Calcutta.','Subject Matter vs Technique Origin':'The captioned Mahavir subject is framed through a popular printed devotional language that includes winged attendants and modelled bodies.'},'popular-print halo-angels jain-iconography',sig='regional-identity=Pune demonstrates another centre of indigenous print production.|religious/cosmological=The named sacred figure is presented through ceremonial enthronement and attendance.|visual/aesthetic=Symmetry, bright colour and multiple frames concentrate attention on the centre.',dist='Mahavir here is identified by the lecture’s title and the represented figure; do not substitute Hanuman merely because Mahavir can be an epithet elsewhere.|The caption does not specify lithography, so keep process provisional.',uncertain=['Exact print process and edition uncaptioned.'])
A('','Relief, intaglio and planography','C',{'Definition':'Relief prints from raised ink-bearing areas; intaglio prints from recessed lines or areas; planography separates image and non-image areas on a substantially flat surface, as in lithography.','Nearest Confusable Terms':'Woodcut is relief; engraving and etching are intaglio; lithography is planographic. The surface carrying ink, not the subject or colour, determines the basic category.','Applied To':'The Sebastian matrix/impression and relief demonstration illustrate raised surfaces; the colonial engravings illustrate intaglio; the stone and diagrams introduce lithography.'},'relief-print engraving etching lithography',dist='The stylized diagram’s protruding greasy marks do not make lithography a relief process.',slides='6:3,4,5,6,7,18')
A('','Chromolithography and hand-colouring','C',{'Definition':'Chromolithography produces colour through successive lithographic printings, requiring alignment. Hand-colouring adds colour manually to an existing impression; a work may combine them.','Nearest Confusable Terms':'A colour woodcut is not a chromolithograph. A hand-tinted chromolithograph is not a contradiction. Misregistration concerns alignment of printed components, not every irregular edge of applied colour.','Applied To':'Buddhadeb and Hawk are captioned chromolithographs; Vijaya is hand-tinted chromolithography; Scene in Bombay uses etching, aquatint and hand-colouring.'},'chromolithography registration',dist='Count neither colours nor matrices from an ordinary slide reproduction: colour mixing and multiple operations prevent a reliable one-to-one inference.',slides='6:17,18,19,20')
A('','Misregistration — analytical study term','C',{'Definition':'Misregistration is unintended misalignment between successive printed components, often visible where one colour shifts relative to another or to a key outline. This term is added from the corpus brief; the lecture slides do not explicitly name it.','Nearest Confusable Terms':'Hand-colouring outside a line, paper distortion, photographic blur and image-compression fringes can resemble misregistration but have different causes.','Applied To':'Colour-print examples in Modules 6 and 7 provide relevant contexts, but this corpus does not certify a registration defect in a particular sheet from the available reproductions.'},'registration',dist='Do not turn this general technical concept into a claim that a particular lecture print is defective without inspecting sufficient evidence.',slides='6:15,16,17,18,19,20;7:1,3,4')
A('','European Print and Indian Bazaar','D',{'Argument':'Printmaking changes knowledge circulation and popular culture while indigenous workshops select technologies, develop imagery and create markets. The module moves from colonial documentation toward local devotional and social imagery.','Evidence':'Landscape engravings, Solvyns’s people and market, Roxburgh’s plant plate, Kalighat–Battala comparisons and Calcutta/Pune studios establish different purposes and production systems.','Course Throughline':'Imported processes do not determine subject matter or authorship; local makers transform reproducible images into regional cultural forms.'},'colonial-documentation popular-print craft-agency',dist='A single story of European invention replacing an image-less India ignores the indigenous textile-printing and painting examples placed early in the module.',slides='6:1,3,7,9,12,13,14,17,18,19,20,21')
